L0000:	db	'This undead fellow looks through you. Though he is obviously'
	db	' aware of his surroundings, you are quite sure that he doesn'
	db	''t even see you.*'
L0089:	db	00
L008A:	db	'Paulette'
L0092:	db	00
L0093:	db	'barmaid'
L009A:	db	00
L009B:	db	'it's not all his fault'
L00B1:	db	00
L00B2:	db	'sacrifice'
L00BB:	db	00
L00BC:	db	'You attempt to start a conversation with the portly ghost, b'
	db	'ut he seems distanced from you and everything else.*'
L012C:	db	00
L012D:	db	'The heavy-set ghost looks a bit shaken and his speech is a l'
	db	'ittle slurred as he says, "Excuse me, yer honor. But I got m'
	db	'e a splittin' headache. Do ya mind if we continue this littl'
	db	'e chat later?"~~He begins to rub his temples with both hands'
	db	'.*'
L021F:	db	00
L0220:	db	'"Oh, hello there, lady Rowena. 'Tis good to see ye again. It'
	db	' brings a ray o' sunshine into this old man's heart ta see y'
	db	'er beauteous face," he says, smiling.*'
L02BE:	db	00
L02BF:	db	'She curtsies delicately and smiles back.~~"Hello, Markham. I'
	db	't is good, indeed, to see that none of this horrible busines'
	db	's can keep thee from giving a lady a compliment."*'
L0369:	db	00
L036A:	db	'"Oh, uh, hello there Mayor. I thought ye were sequestered in'
	db	' the Town Hall. Well, uh, it's good ta see ya again."*'
L03DC:	db	00
L03DD:	db	'"Yes, well, it is good to see thee again, too."*'
L040D:	db	00
L040E:	db	'The corpulent, undead barkeep greets you with a wide, grueso'
	db	'me smile. "Come, stranger. Have a seat near old Markham and '
	db	'tell me o' yer travels. I don't often get visitors in here a'
	db	'nymore."'
L04CA:	db	00
L04CB:	db	'Markham hails you and drinks down a tankard of the house spi'
	db	'rits. "Welcome, my friend. Sit with me a while and enliven m'
	db	'y eternity with yer wondrous wit." He smiles in that charmin'
	db	'g way that only the half-rotted can.'
L05A3:	db	00
L05A4:	db	'bye'
L05A7:	db	00
L05A8:	db	'job'
L05AB:	db	00
L05AC:	db	'name'
L05B0:	db	00
L05B1:	db	'name'
L05B5:	db	00
L05B6:	db	'The heavy-set zombie wipes his mouth off on the back of his '
	db	'hand. "I be Markham. Markham o' the Keg." He pats the large '
	db	'keg of wine he carries.'
L0645:	db	00
L0646:	db	'The lovely '
L0651:	db	00
L0652:	db	' strolls over and pats Markham's rather large belly. "Yes, h'
	db	'e's Markham of the Keg, all right." She smiles sweetly down '
	db	'at the older man.*'
L06DC:	db	00
L06DD:	db	'"That's enough of that!" Markham smacks the pretty young wom'
	db	'an on her ghostly posterior.~~ "Make yerself useful and fetc'
	db	'h me a haunch o' venison." She turns away, giggling. He look'
	db	's at you with a mirthful expression, "I just don't know what'
	db	' I'm goin' ta do with that girl."'
L07EE:	db	00
L07EF:	db	'name'
L07F3:	db	00
L07F4:	db	'job'
L07F7:	db	00
L07F8:	db	'"Why, I run this fine establishment, the Keg O' Spirits." Fo'
	db	'r a moment he becomes serious. "This place once drew folk fr'
	db	'om all across Britannia, gargoyle and human alike. Until the'
	db	' fire, that is."'
L08BC:	db	00
L08BD:	db	'fire'
L08C1:	db	00
L08C2:	db	'Keg O' Spirits'
L08D0:	db	00
L08D1:	db	'fire'
L08D5:	db	00
L08D6:	db	'He looks uncomfortable, "Caine blew the town to the four win'
	db	'ds, and now we're all trapped here, slaves of that bastard H'
	db	'orance." Tiny blue flames appear in the pupils of his glazed'
	db	' eyes, then go out as he regains his composure.'
L09B9:	db	00
L09BA:	db	'Horance'
L09C1:	db	00
L09C2:	db	'Caine'
L09C7:	db	00
L09C8:	db	'"Please, Markham. Have a little pity for Caine. He was tryin'
	db	'g to create something to save the town when he made his fata'
	db	'l mistake." The pale ghost looks deeply troubled.'
L0A71:	db	00
L0A72:	db	'yer right Quen,'
L0A81:	db	00
L0A82:	db	'"Oh, I suppose '
L0A91:	db	00
L0A92:	db	' he was tryin' ta help us when he called the proverbial fire'
	db	's o' Hell down on us. It just rankles me to have died in my '
	db	'prime." His roguish smile once again lightens his ghostly vi'
	db	'sage.'
L0B4B:	db	00
L0B4C:	db	'fire'
L0B50:	db	00
L0B51:	db	'Caine'
L0B56:	db	00
L0B57:	db	'A look of disgust comes to his disfigured features. "That to'
	db	'rtured soul haunts the crater made by his foolish mistake. I'
	db	' wouldn't go near him though, he's a bit daft, ya know." He '
	db	'refills his mug from the cask at his side and swigs down mos'
	db	't of the wine in one swallow.'
L0C64:	db	00
L0C65:	db	'tortured soul'
L0C72:	db	00
L0C73:	db	'Caine'
L0C78:	db	00
L0C79:	db	'tortured soul'
L0C86:	db	00
L0C87:	db	'"That's just the name the rest of us in Skara Brae call him '
	db	'-- the Tortured One," he grins, embarrassed.'
L0CEF:	db	00
L0CF0:	db	'tortured soul'
L0CFD:	db	00
L0CFE:	db	'Skara Brae'
L0D08:	db	00
L0D09:	db	'Skara Brae'
L0D13:	db	00
L0D14:	db	'"That's the name o' the island yer on." He shakes his head.'
L0D4F:	db	00
L0D50:	db	'Skara Brae'
L0D5A:	db	00
L0D5B:	db	'Horance'
L0D62:	db	00
L0D63:	db	'"For all the years I've been in Skara Brae, he's been a ravi'
	db	'ng lunatic. What with all o' them silly rhymes and his crazy'
	db	' laughter.~~"Then one night, we all hears thunder when there'
	db	' isn't a cloud in the starry sky, and I seem to recall a ful'
	db	'l moon..." He gets a thoughtful look on his face. "But as I '
	db	'was sayin', there was this thunder, then this deep, dark lau'
	db	'ghter coming from the tower on the northern point -- Horance'
	db	''s Dark Tower." After this he falls silent for a moment.'
L0F3F:	db	00
L0F40:	db	'The pale ghost moves forward and whispers, "I was already li'
	db	'ving in the half world of the dead when these events took pl'
	db	'ace, and ever since, I've felt a strange pull coming from th'
	db	'e tower."*'
L0FFE:	db	00
L0FFF:	db	'After a brief swig, he continues, "Then, even worse... I'm o'
	db	'ut checkin' on the cows when I hears a sound like moanin'. I'
	db	't's off to the east, so I look that way, into the graveyard '
	db	'y'know, and what do I see?~~"I'll tell ya what I seen. The g'
	db	'raves, rippin' open like the people in 'em got a place to go'
	db	'." Eyes wide, he tips back another sip.'
L1152:	db	00
L1153:	db	'Horance'
L115A:	db	00
L115B:	db	'Keg O' Spirits'
L1169:	db	00
L116A:	db	'He truly looks sad as he says, "This place was once my pride'
	db	' and joy. The Keg was known all 'round Britannia, and a few '
	db	'other places, too. Well, now it don't look like too much, bu'
	db	't in it's heyday, it saw the likes of nobles, knights, minst'
	db	'rels, and merchants. And o' course, a bit o' riff raff to be'
	db	' sure." He winks at you. His spirit seems indomitable.'
L12CC:	db	00
L12CD:	db	'Keg O' Spirits'
L12DB:	db	00
L12DC:	db	'sacrifice'
L12E5:	db	00
L12E6:	db	'You relate the need for a sacrifice to enter the Well of Sou'
	db	'ls. Afterwards, Markham seems to think long and hard.~~"So, '
	db	'yer wantin' me to go mad as a March hare, an' jump right int'
	db	'o this... Well O' Souls?" He looks at you incredulously.~~"L'
	db	'isten now. I haven't had courage like that since I were a yo'
	db	'ung lad. Since then I got some sense, too. You'll have to lo'
	db	'ok elsewhere fer yer sacrifice."'
L146E:	db	00
L146F:	db	'"All right, now. I already told ya. I ain't interested." He '
	db	'looks a little put out by your persistence.*'
L14D7:	db	00
L14D8:	db	'sacrifice'
L14E1:	db	00
L14E2:	db	'bye'
L14E5:	db	00
L14E6:	db	'"Oh, are ya leavin' then? Well, ya take care now. And watch '
	db	'out for those walkin' dead. Some o' them aren't too happy ab'
	db	'out their state, and none too picky about who they complain '
	db	'to, neither."*'
