		.funcnumber	0492H
		.data
L0000:	db	'The pale ghost seems to see you but cannot speak to you for '
	db	'some reason. In frustration the ghost turns away.*'
L006E:	db	00
L006F:	db	'Markham'
L0076:	db	00
L0077:	db	'The barkeep'
L0082:	db	00
L0083:	db	'sacrifice'
L008C:	db	00
L008D:	db	'As you start to speak to the pale ghost, you notice that he '
	db	'seems to be looking through you, as if you don't exist at al'
	db	'l. You wave your hand in front of his face, but there is no '
	db	'response.*'
L014B:	db	00
L014C:	db	'"Please, please. I... cannot speak with thee right now. I am'
	db	' not sure what has come over me. Please forgive me, '
L01BC:	db	00
L01BD:	db	'." The wan ghost looks more pale than usual.*'
L01EA:	db	00
L01EB:	db	'"Hello, Quenton. I hope thou art doing well." Rowena gives t'
	db	'he pale ghost a winning smile.*'
L0246:	db	00
L0247:	db	'"Yes, milady. I am doing as well as can be expected. It glad'
	db	'dens mine heart to see that thou art once again free. Hast t'
	db	'hou been to see Trent yet?"*'
L02DB:	db	00
L02DC:	db	'"Alas, no. This kind person is taking me to him." She indica'
	db	'tes you.*'
L0321:	db	00
L0322:	db	'"These are glad tidings, for he misses thee so."*'
L0353:	db	00
L0354:	db	'"Well met, Quenton." The Mayor's mustache spreads as he smil'
	db	'es.*'
L0394:	db	00
L0395:	db	'"Hello, Mayor. How dost thou fare, milord?"*'
L03C1:	db	00
L03C2:	db	'Forsythe seems taken aback by Quenton's sincere sounding que'
	db	'ry. "Why, I fare well, Quenton. I thank thee for thy concern'
	db	'."*'
L043D:	db	00
L043E:	db	'He smiles in acknowledgement of the Mayor's thanks.*'
L0472:	db	00
L0473:	db	'The pale-looking ghost turns in your direction and gives you'
	db	' a wan smile. "Hello, could it be that we have met somewhere'
	db	' before, '
L04F4:	db	00
L04F5:	db	'?" You see recognition in his eyes, then it fades.~~"Forgive'
	db	' me." He shakes his head, then smiles. "I am the shade of Qu'
	db	'enton."'
L0574:	db	00
L0575:	db	'Quenton turns in your direction. "Greetings, '
L05A2:	db	00
L05A3:	db	'. Come, take rest from thy travels and sit a while with me. '
	db	'I am but a simple shade, but I may have information useful t'
	db	'o thee."'
L0623:	db	00
L0624:	db	'information'
L062F:	db	00
L0630:	db	'bye'
L0633:	db	00
L0634:	db	'shade'
L0639:	db	00
L063A:	db	'job'
L063D:	db	00
L063E:	db	'name'
L0642:	db	00
L0643:	db	'Tortured One'
L064F:	db	00
L0650:	db	'name'
L0654:	db	00
L0655:	db	'"I am called Quenton, '
L066B:	db	00
L066C:	db	'."'
L066E:	db	00
L066F:	db	'name'
L0673:	db	00
L0674:	db	'job'
L0677:	db	00
L0678:	db	'He smiles at your question, "I once roamed the sea, for days'
	db	' at a time, gathering mine harvest of fish."'
L06E0:	db	00
L06E1:	db	'information'
L06EC:	db	00
L06ED:	db	'"I have been around for many, many years. And," he smiles, "'
	db	'I have seen many, many things in that time."'
L0755:	db	00
L0756:	db	'information'
L0761:	db	00
L0762:	db	'Tortured One'
L076E:	db	00
L076F:	db	'"Caine? He was an alchemist here on Skara Brae. Now he spend'
	db	's his days in eternal pain caused by his guilt from causing '
	db	'the fire that destroyed this town."'
L080A:	db	00
L080B:	db	'Tortured One'
L0817:	db	00
L0818:	db	'shade'
L081D:	db	00
L081E:	db	'"My story is a long and a sad one. I hope thou hast some tim'
	db	'e." He appears thoughtful for a moment, and then begins.~~ ['
	db	'"When I was a young man, I met a lovely woman by the name of'
	db	' Gwen. I made her my wife, and we lived for a time, happy an'
	db	'd carefree. She brought a light into the world and we called'
	db	' her Marney, which means the cool breeze after a storm." He '
	db	'smiles to himself at some memory, then continues with a furr'
	db	'owed brow.'
L09CC:	db	00
L09CD:	db	'"Then, one day, my wife was taken from me. I know not where,'
	db	' or by whom, save that they were evil men. Soon after, my sw'
	db	'eet Marney became sick at heart and I feared for her health.'
	db	' I could not take time from my fishing to care for her, but '
	db	'I needed gold. So I made a deal with a man who was not to be'
	db	' trifled with. This was mine undoing, for when I failed to r'
	db	'epay his loan, he came to me one night and slew me. I had no'
	db	't a chance to fight back or call for help." He falls silent.'
	db	'~~"But that was long before the fire that turned this whole '
	db	'island into the land of the dead."'
L0C0B:	db	00
L0C0C:	db	'shade'
L0C11:	db	00
L0C12:	db	'fire'
L0C16:	db	00
L0C17:	db	'Marney'
L0C1D:	db	00
L0C1E:	db	'Marney'
L0C24:	db	00
L0C25:	db	'"After I was murdered, my good friend, Yorl, cared for her a'
	db	's his own. He tried his best, but her sickness only worsened'
	db	'. After several months she weakened, and died." He stops her'
	db	'e, tears filling his ghostly eyes, then, angrily, he says, "'
	db	'And now her spirit is held by Horance the Liche. Thou must r'
	db	'escue her from that foul beast!" He attempts to grab you, bu'
	db	't his hands pass through without resistance.*'
L0DBA:	db	00
L0DBB:	db	'"Now, now, Quen. Settle down." '
L0DDA:	db	00
L0DDB:	db	' moves closer to you and whispers, "Fergive him, '
L0E0C:	db	00
L0E0D:	db	'.~~"He sometimes loses control like that when he talks about'
	db	' his daughter. Sure'n ya can understand, tho'."*'
L0E79:	db	00
L0E7A:	db	'Quenton regains control of himself. "Forgive me, '
L0EAB:	db	00
L0EAC:	db	'. I've no right to inflict my woes upon thee. It hurts to th'
	db	'ink of my sweet Marney in the power of that... creature."'
L0F21:	db	00
L0F22:	db	'Marney'
L0F28:	db	00
L0F29:	db	'fire'
L0F2D:	db	00
L0F2E:	db	'"It seems that Mistress Mordra, the town healer, thought she'
	db	' had a plan to stop the Liche, Horance, which she told to th'
	db	'e Mayor. I am not sure exactly what she planned, but it invo'
	db	'lved Trent, the town smith, and Caine, the alchemist. Not lo'
	db	'ng after Caine began his work, a maelstrom of fire tore over'
	db	' the island, destroying everything. Skara Brae burned for da'
	db	'ys."'
L109A:	db	00
L109B:	db	'fire'
L109F:	db	00
L10A0:	db	'Caine'
L10A5:	db	00
L10A6:	db	'Trent'
L10AB:	db	00
L10AC:	db	'Mayor'
L10B1:	db	00
L10B2:	db	'Liche'
L10B7:	db	00
L10B8:	db	'Mistress Mordra'
L10C7:	db	00
L10C8:	db	'Liche'
L10CD:	db	00
L10CE:	db	'"Once, over two centuries ago, I knew a gifted mage named Ho'
	db	'rance. His two loves in life were the study of magic, and wr'
	db	'iting lovely poetry. The people of Skara Brae felt safe in t'
	db	'he knowledge that this sort of mage protected the town. Then'
	db	' he began to change.~~"First his beautiful sonnets became a '
	db	'rhyming doggerel. It became the only way in which he would s'
	db	'peak. His spells, which he displayed before the townsfolk, b'
	db	'ecame destructive and violent. People began to fear him. My '
	db	'death occurred at about this time. Not long after that, he b'
	db	'ecame reclusive. He had a tower built on the northern point '
	db	'and never removed himself from it.~~ "Then, one night, the g'
	db	'raves in the graveyard opened and the dead began to walk."*'
L139D:	db	00
L139E:	db	' nods his head emphatically, "'At's right, I seen it, I did.'
	db	'"*'
L13DC:	db	00
L13DD:	db	'"They marched to his tower, and now they roam all over the i'
	db	'sland, performing his bidding."*'
L1439:	db	00
L143A:	db	'"It be gettin' so's a ghost cannot make an honest livin' no '
	db	'more. Hmph." '
L1483:	db	00
L1484:	db	' looks a bit disgruntled.*'
L149E:	db	00
L149F:	db	'Liche'
L14A4:	db	00
L14A5:	db	'Mistress Mordra'
L14B4:	db	00
L14B5:	db	'Quenton looks hopeful, "If thou wouldst like to assist us, s'
	db	'he is the best one to speak to. She seems to know the way to'
	db	' rid us of the Liche, at the least."'
L1551:	db	00
L1552:	db	'Mistress Mordra'
L1561:	db	00
L1562:	db	'Mayor'
L1567:	db	00
L1568:	db	'"The mayor...," Quenton selects his words carefully. "...wel'
	db	'l, he believes that discretion is the better part of Valor. '
	db	'So, he may be able to offer thee some aid, but thou art like'
	db	'ly first to need convince him that thou'rt not here to hurt '
	db	'him."'
L165D:	db	00
L165E:	db	'Mayor'
L1663:	db	00
L1664:	db	'Trent'
L1669:	db	00
L166A:	db	'"Ah, the poor man knows the spirit-wrenching feeling of loss'
	db	' almost as well as I. His wife, Rowena, was killed by the wa'
	db	'lking dead. And Mistress Mordra claims that she saw her sitt'
	db	'ing on a throne next to the Liche's own. I believe this has '
	db	'driven Trent somewhat mad. He works night and day upon some '
	db	'oddly formed cage. Strange, though, he never seems to finish'
	db	' it. He doth not seem to recall that he died in the fire, ei'
	db	'ther, but a great hatred for Horance still burns in his hear'
	db	't."'
L184D:	db	00
L184E:	db	'Trent'
L1853:	db	00
L1854:	db	'Caine'
L1859:	db	00
L185A:	db	'He looks as if he expected your question. "Alas, Caine, in h'
	db	'is attempt to free us of the Liche, instead damned us to bec'
	db	'ome slaves of the selfsame Liche."'
L18F4:	db	00
L18F5:	db	'Caine'
L18FA:	db	00
L18FB:	db	'slaves'
L1901:	db	00
L1902:	db	'slaves'
L1908:	db	00
L1909:	db	'"Yes, we are his slaves. Every night at midnight, we must go'
	db	' to the Dark Tower and become servants of his Black Mass. I '
	db	'only know this because Mordra tells us it is so. I have no r'
	db	'ecollection of ever having been to the Dark Tower at all." H'
	db	'is expression betrays his fear.'
L1A18:	db	00
L1A19:	db	'slaves'
L1A1F:	db	00
L1A20:	db	'sacrifice'
L1A29:	db	00
L1A2A:	db	'You explain that you need a spirit to volunteer to freely en'
	db	'ter the Well of Souls in order to bring about its destructio'
	db	'n. Quenton considers for a while, and then responds, "Please'
	db	' understand, '
L1AEB:	db	00
L1AEC:	db	'. I truly wish that I had that kind of Courage. But I cannot'
	db	' risk doing anything that might destroy Marney. Remember, he'
	db	'r spirit is kept in that well, along with all of the dead of'
	db	' the graveyard."'
L1BB0:	db	00
L1BB1:	db	'"No, I am sorry. I cannot risk it." He looks very weary.'
L1BE9:	db	00
L1BEA:	db	'sacrifice'
L1BF3:	db	00
L1BF4:	db	'bye'
L1BF7:	db	00
L1BF8:	db	'"Goodbye, '
L1C02:	db	00
L1C03:	db	'."*'
L1C06:	db	00
L1C07:	db	'He turns back to his conversation with '
L1C2E:	db	00
L1C2F:	db	'.*'
