		.data
L0000:	db	'bye'
L0003:	db	00
L0004:	db	'job'
L0007:	db	00
L0008:	db	'name'
L000C:	db	00
L000D:	db	'The woman greets you with a curtsey.'
L0031:	db	00
L0032:	db	'"Good day, '
L003D:	db	00
L003E:	db	'," says Lady Jehanne.'
L0053:	db	00
L0054:	db	'commons'
L005B:	db	00
L005C:	db	'name'
L0060:	db	00
L0061:	db	'"I am the Lady Jehanne, '
L0079:	db	00
L007A:	db	'."'
L007C:	db	00
L007D:	db	'name'
L0081:	db	00
L0082:	db	'commons'
L0089:	db	00
L008A:	db	'job'
L008D:	db	00
L008E:	db	'"I am the provisioner of Serpent's Hold."'
L00B7:	db	00
L00B8:	db	'"And," she adds, "I also have a ship for sale shouldst thou '
	db	'be interested in that."'
L010B:	db	00
L010C:	db	'ship'
L0110:	db	00
L0111:	db	'provisions'
L011B:	db	00
L011C:	db	'Serpent's Hold'
L012A:	db	00
L012B:	db	'ship'
L012F:	db	00
L0130:	db	'"Well, it was once the magnificent `Constellation.' However,'
	db	' 'twas destroyed by the ship's captain, himself, to prevent '
	db	'it from falling into the hands of attacking pirates. What li'
	db	'ttle remained was rebuilt into an even finer ship, `The Drag'
	db	'on's Breath?' Art thou interested in purchasing it for 600 g'
	db	'old?"'
L0261:	db	00
L0262:	db	'"Here is thy deed."'
L0275:	db	00
L0276:	db	'"Sadly, '
L027E:	db	00
L027F:	db	', thou hast not the room for this deed."'
L02A7:	db	00
L02A8:	db	'"I understand, '
L02B7:	db	00
L02B8:	db	', thou hast not the funds at this time."'
L02E0:	db	00
L02E1:	db	'"Perhaps sometime in the future, '
L0302:	db	00
L0303:	db	'."'
L0305:	db	00
L0306:	db	'ship'
L030A:	db	00
L030B:	db	'Serpent's Hold'
L0319:	db	00
L031A:	db	'"Most of us here are knights, noble warriors sworn to protec'
	db	't Britannia and Lord British. Mine own lord," she beams with'
	db	' pride, "is such a knight -- Sir Pendaran."'
L03BD:	db	00
L03BE:	db	'Serpent's Hold'
L03CC:	db	00
L03CD:	db	'knights'
L03D4:	db	00
L03D5:	db	'Sir Pendaran'
L03E1:	db	00
L03E2:	db	'Sir Pendaran'
L03EE:	db	00
L03EF:	db	'"We met three years ago. He's quite brave and strong. I just'
	db	' love to watch him fight." She smiles.~~"I am not really sur'
	db	'e he will mix well with the rest of The Fellowship, though."'
L04A3:	db	00
L04A4:	db	'Sir Pendaran'
L04B0:	db	00
L04B1:	db	'Fellowship'
L04BB:	db	00
L04BC:	db	'fight'
L04C1:	db	00
L04C2:	db	'fight'
L04C7:	db	00
L04C8:	db	'"He and Menion used to spar together, after their exercises.'
	db	' 'Twas a beautiful... sight, '
L0521:	db	00
L0522:	db	'," she says, blushing.'
L0538:	db	00
L0539:	db	'fight'
L053E:	db	00
L053F:	db	'used to'
L0546:	db	00
L0547:	db	'used to'
L054E:	db	00
L054F:	db	'"At the time, Pendaran was the only man who could keep up wi'
	db	'th Menion. Now that Menion has begun instructing others, he '
	db	'no longer has the time to practice with my Lord."'
L05F8:	db	00
L05F9:	db	'used to'
L0600:	db	00
L0601:	db	'Fellowship'
L060B:	db	00
L060C:	db	'"Well, er, I mean, he would not have mixed well -before- he '
	db	'joined, that is," she stammers.'
L0667:	db	00
L0668:	db	'"It's nothing really. He was just a little bit more... indiv'
	db	'idualistic before he joined. I do not think there's anything'
	db	' wrong with The Fellowship, necessarily; but I did not expec'
	db	't it to be something that would capture Pendaran's interest.'
	db	'"'
L0759:	db	00
L075A:	db	'Fellowship'
L0764:	db	00
L0765:	db	'knights'
L076C:	db	00
L076D:	db	'With but a few exceptions, myself included, all of the warri'
	db	'ors here in the Hold are knights. Thou mayest wish to speak '
	db	'with Lord John-Paul. He is in charge of Serpent's Hold and m'
	db	'ight be better able to show thee around.'
L0849:	db	00
L084A:	db	'knights'
L0851:	db	00
L0852:	db	'provisions'
L085C:	db	00
L085D:	db	'"Thou wishest to buy something?"'
L087D:	db	00
L087E:	db	'"Well, perhaps next time, '
L0898:	db	00
L0899:	db	'."'
L089B:	db	00
L089C:	db	'"A better time to buy would be when my shop is open."'
L08D1:	db	00
L08D2:	db	'provisions'
L08DC:	db	00
L08DD:	db	'commons'
L08E4:	db	00
L08E5:	db	'For an instant, you see indecisiveness in her expression, th'
	db	'en she suddenly gives in, her words coming out in a torrent '
	db	'of information.~~"I am afraid to speak, but knowing thou wou'
	db	'ldst see through any facade, I can no longer silence the tru'
	db	'th. My Lord, Sir Pendaran, has not been the same gentle soul'
	db	' since he joined the Fellowship.~~"'Twas not too long ago th'
	db	'at my Pendaran was a noble knight, one a lady could be proud'
	db	' of. But now," she shakes her head, "in protest of a wrong h'
	db	'e perceives in Britannia's government, he has defaced the st'
	db	'atue of our beloved Lord British." She begins to sob.~~"And,'
	db	' he has battled and wounded a fellow knight who chanced upon'
	db	' him during his hour of misdeed. He came to me," she tries t'
	db	'o choke back her tears, "with another's blood on his sword!"'
	db	'~~After a few moments of your comforting, she regains her co'
	db	'mposure.~~"Please do not be too harsh with him," she begs.'
